          "distinct": true,
          "id": "c588adcbceb3cd863ec4625ab0b276bb53c41b43",
          "message": "test(js): JS-side laziness/memory test for Source.match / matchStream (sq-y9v8n) [OPUS-4.8] (#1244)\n\n@jeswr's review thread #1 on PR #1143 asked for an N3.js-style memory test for\nthe lazy `match` refactor — that thread was resolved on the refactor alone\nwithout the test landing. This adds it.\n\n`test/source-match-laziness.test.mjs` asserts the laziness that is GENUINELY\ntrue on the JS side after the refactor (`Source.match` → `matchStream` →\n`queryBindingsStream`, pulling the engine's SPARQL-JSON in ~64 KiB chunks):\n\n  (a) `matchStream()` yields the first quad after pulling exactly ONE solution\n      (a chunk-pull spy on `queryBindingsStream` proves it; does not drain all N);\n  (b) advancing the generator pulls one solution per advance — it never buffers\n      the whole `Quad[]` on the JS side, unlike eager `match()` (`[...matchStream]`);\n  (c) an early `break` stops pulling (no over-fetch) and leaves the store usable\n      (the wasm cursor is freed in the generator's `finally`);\n  (d) the event-based `Source.match(...)` Stream surfaces the first quad via\n      `read()` before the result is drained, then emits data-per-quad then end.\n\nHONESTY — what it deliberately does NOT claim: there is no TRUE\n\"too-large-to-materialise\" guarantee end-to-end. The wasm side\n(`Store.query_chunks` → `query_json_chunks_with_budget`) builds the FULL chunk\nsequence eagerly into a `Vec<String>` before the first `cursor.next()` — no\nback-pressure to the engine — so the result must still fit in wasm memory. The\nstreaming win is JS-side only. The file/test names + a header comment state this,\nand the wasm-back-pressure gap is tracked in sq-y9v8n.\n\nSelf-contained (SparqStore/SparqSource only); `npm test` green (the 2 unrelated\nfailures are external-harness packages not installed in this env). typecheck clean.\n\n🤖 SPARQ agent\n\nCo-authored-by: Jesse Wright <jmwright.045@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
